nato policy a return to deterrence in  the interview nATO Secretary General  Anders Fogh Rasmussen mr. Rasmussen your  five-year term at the helm of NATO ends  in a couple of weeks time if you strike  your personal balance mission  accomplished at least I'm watching  developments in in Europe putting more  focus on NATO and making it obvious to  everybody that we need a very strong  NATO to provide effective defense  protection of our allies so compared to  five years ago I think we see today a  much stronger fitter faster NATO when  you hand over businesses nato secretary  general to your successor what will you  tell them what is currently the biggest  challenge for native the biggest  challenge will be to reinforce our  collective defense because we are faced  with a dramatically new security  situation in Europe Russia's illegal  military actions in Ukraine are a  wake-up call and a reminder that we  cannot take our security for granted so  the biggest challenge right now is to  keep our line strong to reinforce our  collective defence and that would also  take more investment in our security I  would like to talk a bit more about this  security crisis in Europe five years ago  your key goal was to restore the ties to  Russia the former enemy to re-engage  Russia this was your slogan what went  wrong yeah still I think it was the  right thing to do  actually not only during the last five  years but for more than 20 years we have  tried to engage Russia in a constructive  cooperation and did from my heart I  still believe that the right thing is an  engagement with Russia and basically I  think we share interests in in many  areas but I have to realize that the  Russian thinking is different and though  we decided back in 2010 to develop a  true strategic partnership between NATO  and Russia and Russia doesn't behave as  a partner and we can see from Russian  military document that the Russians  consider NATO an adversary and of course  we have to adapt to that but still I  think it was the right thing to do to  try to engage a Russia but we have to  realize that Russia sees it differently  and we will adapt to that adapting to  the new situation which words would you  use to describe what Russia is now is it  a strategic rival or the new old enemy  definitely not a partner and we see from  the Russian doctrines that they consider  us an adversary but I mean the back in  97 we adopted a joint nato-russia  document called the founding act in that  founding act we stated that we do not  consider each other adversaries and we  will not use force against eat each  other but we have now seen in Ukraine  that Russia is in a blatant breach of  all these fundamental principles so I  think for the foreseeable future we will  have to engage with Russia from a  position of strength and unity and it  was discussing right now what to do in  Crete means are you in favor of  deploying combat troops substantial  troops in countries like Poland or the  three Baltic states and we have made one  thing clear right from the outset that  we stick to the nato-russia founding act  from 1997 and we hope Russia will also  fulfill its commitments within that  founding act having said that I have no  doubt that we need a more visible  presence all over NATO territory  including in on the territory of our  Eastern allies but whatever we do it  will be in compliance with the  nato-russia founding act though Russia  herself has annulled this founding act  of 97 not formally but you're right in  reality Russia has violated the  fundamental principles of the  nato-russia founding act but that  doesn't change our position we are in  favor of a rules-based international  order and also a rules-based security  order in the euro-atlantic area so we  stick to the principles of the  nato-russia founding act and the  measures we are going to take will be in  accordance with this founding act but  NATO's looking that's my impression for  a new long-term approach towards Russia  what do you personally recommend when  you leave office in a few weeks time  should NATO adopt a strategy of  containment em I don't believe in in  containment and I have heard this  Russian suspicion of being encircled by  NATO allies and and and that's really  not a the truth it's not the reality on  the contrary NATO does not constitute a  threat  against Russia I have seen that Russia  considers our open-door policy and  enlargements of NATO as a threat towards  Russia but it's not a threat towards  roster it's based on the fundamental  principle that each and every nation has  the right to decide herself its security  policies and its alliance affiliations  even Russia has subscribed to that  principle and Russia has profited from  enlargements of NATO and the European  Union because thanks to those  enlargement we have created a zone of  stability peace and prosperity in  Eastern and Central Europe and if you  have a look at the trade figures and  investment figures economic figures in  general you will see that Russia has  actually profited from that shown of the  stability and prosperity Western  military experts are still struggling to  find a word for what Russia is doing in  Ukraine right now there's talk of hybrid  warfare or ambiguous warfare what  exactly does that mean yeah it means a  combination of traditional military  measures and more covert military  operations combined with sophisticated  of information and disinformation  operations and that's exactly what we  have seen in Crimea for instance you saw  these so-called green men without  possibility to identify their  nationality so that was a kind of covert  military operation in Crimea which was  supported by a proper as I would say a  true propaganda war and eventually what  I would call a fake referendum and  eventually an illegal annexation of  Crimea  that's a new type of warfare we call it  hybrid warfare because it's a  combination of well-known and new  methods my impression was that NATO was  rather unprepared to this new type of  warfare how come and of course some  lessons have been learned I have to  confess it was a surprise to us that  Russia would violate all the fundamental  principles of the nato-russia founding  documents but also violate its  international commitments and  international law in general we had  expected Russia to respect those  fundamental principles and we have  operated during the last more than 20  years under the assumption that Russia  shared our view that we need a  rules-based international order and  security system but we have to realize  that they think differently and now we  have to adapt to that new situation if  the Russian leadership decided to test  NATO in the Baltic state could they be  defended on the article 5 I can assure  you that we will take all measures  necessary to ensure effective defense of  all allies article 5 in our treaty is  essential it states that an attack on  one would be considered an attack on all  so if any potential aggressor were to  attack a NATO ally all other NATO allies  would help that specific ally and  provides effective defense and  protection finally a brief look  at the ongoing war in Syria and the  onslaught of Isis in Iraq and as well  the Middle East as we know it seems to  be falling apart in how far does this  affect NATO and the Western alliance  potentially it can affect our alliance  in particular our ally Turkey and you  have seen us take the first steps we  have deployed Patriot missiles to Turkey  to ensure effective defense of turkey  against potential missile attacks from  Syria and of course we follow the  situation in the region closely with a  particular view to ensure effective  defense of our ally Turkey nATO  Secretary General Anders Fogh Rasmussen  thank you very much for joining us at  Deutsche Welle you're welcome thank you
 

